   206	\paragraph{Prospective replication on new markets.} After two external reviews of this section, we planned a replication before any of its outcomes existed: the plan, its five expectations, the analysis code, and its tests were committed, timestamped with OpenTimestamps, and published, and six new markets (rep0 to rep5) were then drawn from a later Bitcoin block by the second test's market generator. The replication runs R3, C$^*$, B3, and the runner-up comparator C-thr(\const{12}) at $p = \const{0}$, \const{0.03}, and \const{0.05}, with D2+H3 attacks searched on the new markets, each rule's selected no-churn reference candidate replayed under the same churn, the registered evaluation and doubling check in every cell, and continued search of up to three rounds, under the same churn, for every cell whose doubling check was flagged. Of the \const{72} cells, \site{rp_cont} were flagged and continued (\site{rp_cont0} at $p = \const{0}$, \site{rp_cont3} at \const{0.03}, and \site{rp_cont5} at \const{0.05}; \site{rp_cont_r3} of them R3's), and every continued cell passed the continuation check within three rounds; passing that check is not a claim of convergence. The primary analysis uses each cell's frozen winner unless its doubling check was flagged, and the continued winner otherwise; the all-frozen set is a sensitivity analysis. The support criterion for a contrast at a rate is descriptive: its mean paired change from the same market's no-churn value is below zero, and the change is negative on at least five of the six markets; it is not a significance test. Its material companion requires a mean change at or below the registered Q1 margin of $-\const{0.05}$; both are reported for both winner sets. The fourth expectation carried a stated prospective probability of \const{0.5}. For the fifth, R3's audited share of the attacker's jobs is the number of the attacker's audited jobs over its served jobs, each summed over a market's \const{200} evaluation seeds; a market where the attacker serves no job at either rate counts as not lower. Seed-bootstrap intervals are \const{95}\% paired percentile intervals from \const{9999} resamples (seed \const{0}), resampling seeds within each market; market $t$ intervals use \const{5} degrees of freedom. We call the replication prospective-exploratory: its plan was fixed and stamped before its markets existed, but it is not part of either registered test and changes no registered decision.
   207	
   208	\paragraph{Replication results.} Table~\ref{tab:replication} gives the paired changes and the absolute contrasts; Appendix~\ref{sec:app-rep} gives them per market, with the attack and reference terms, the efficiency of every rule, and R3's audit allocation. With the primary winners all five expectations are met: the support criterion holds for C$^*$ minus R3 at $p = \const{0.05}$ (``\site{rp_e1}''), for B3 minus R3 at \const{0.05} (``\site{rp_e2}''), for both at \const{0.03} (``\site{rp_e3}''), and for the runner-up minus R3 at \const{0.05} (``\site{rp_e4}''), and R3's audited share of the attacker's jobs is lower at \const{0.05} than without churn on at least five of six markets (``\site{rp_e5}''). The mean paired changes are \site{rp_d5} ($d$), \site{rp_q5} ($q$), and \site{rp_u5} ($u$) at $p = \const{0.05}$, and \site{rp_d3}, \site{rp_q3}, and \site{rp_u3} at \const{0.03}. The material companion holds for \site{rp_mat} of the six contrast and rate pairs with the primary winners and for \site{rpf_mat} with the all-frozen winners; the support criterion holds for \site{rp_sup} and \site{rpf_sup}. These means rest mainly on two markets: at $p = \const{0.05}$ with the primary winners, $d$ changes by \site{rp_d5_t0} and \site{rp_d5_t1} on rep0 and rep1, and by between \site{rp_d5_rlo} and \site{rp_d5_rhi} on the other four; with the primary winners the other contrasts and the rate \const{0.03} show the same pattern (Table~\ref{tab:rep-markets}). The material companion therefore describes the mean change, not a typical market. With the primary winners, the only market $t$ interval of a mean change that excludes zero is that of B3 minus R3 at \const{0.05} ([\site{rp_q5_lo}, \site{rp_q5_hi}]); with the all-frozen winners the three intervals at \const{0.05} exclude zero and none at \const{0.03} does. With the all-frozen winners, the first, second, fourth, and fifth expectations are met (``\site{rpf_e1}'', ``\site{rpf_e2}'', ``\site{rpf_e4}'', ``\site{rpf_e5}''), and the third is ``\site{rpf_e3}'': C$^*$ minus R3 at \const{0.03} is lower than without churn on only \site{rpf_d3_k} of six markets, so it fails the support criterion while meeting the material companion. Continued search moved both the no-churn baselines and the churn cells: the pooled C$^*$ minus R3 difference with the primary winners minus that with the all-frozen winners is \site{rp_sd0} without churn, \site{rp_sd3} at $p = \const{0.03}$, and \site{rp_sd5} at \const{0.05}. With the all-frozen winners the mean paired changes at \const{0.05} are \site{rpf_d5} ($d$), \site{rpf_q5} ($q$), and \site{rpf_u5} ($u$), further from zero than with the primary winners: at this rate the better-searched winner set gives the weaker changes. The paired changes therefore carry the search uncertainty of the baselines as well as that of the churn cells. As planned, the failed expectation is reported without a causal reading.
   209	
   210	\paragraph{Ordering and audits on the new markets.} At $p = \const{0.05}$ with the primary winners, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{rp_ad5} and negative on \site{rp_ad5_n} of six markets, the runner-up minus R3 is \site{rp_au5} (negative on \site{rp_au5_n}), and B3 minus R3 is \site{rp_aq5}, negative on \site{rp_aq5_n} markets (\site{rpf_aq5_n} with the all-frozen winners). The sign reversals of the earlier follow-up thus recur on the new markets in point estimates, but with the primary winners all three market $t$ intervals of these absolute contrasts include zero (Table~\ref{tab:replication}); the pooled seed-bootstrap interval of B3 minus R3, which holds the six markets fixed, is [\site{rp_aq5_blo}, \site{rp_aq5_bhi}] (Table~\ref{tab:rep-absolute}). At $p = \const{0.03}$, C$^*$ minus R3 is \site{rp_ad3} ([\site{rp_ad3_lo}, \site{rp_ad3_hi}]) and negative on only \site{rp_ad3_n} of six markets (\site{rpf_ad3_n} with the all-frozen winners), against all six under both search versions in the earlier follow-up; the new markets do not repeat that result. R3's audited share of the attacker's jobs falls from between \site{rp_fa0_lo} and \site{rp_fa0_hi} without churn to between \site{rp_fa5_lo} and \site{rp_fa5_hi} at $p = \const{0.05}$, and is lower on \site{rp_fa_below} of six markets. At $p = \const{0.05}$, honest identities younger than ten rounds serve \site{rp_yj5} of the jobs and receive \site{rp_ya5} of R3's audits, and established honest jobs are audited at \site{rp_est5} (equal-weight means over the six markets; Table~\ref{tab:rep-alloc}). At $p = \const{0.03}$ the primary allocation covers only \site{rp_al3_n} of six markets, because the plan ran diagnostics for continued R3 winners only at $p = \const{0}$ and \const{0.05}. Under its selected attack at \const{0.05}, R3's mean gross efficiency is \site{rp_eff5} (lowest market \site{rp_eff5_min}) and C$^*$'s is \site{rp_effc5} (Table~\ref{tab:rep-terms}).
   211	
   212	\paragraph{Scope.} The earlier follow-up ran one verified-history comparator under churn; the replication adds the runner-up, and on the new markets the deterioration under its descriptive criterion is not specific to C$^*$. Every claim is restricted to the generator's market neighbourhood, the four rules, the D2+H3 family, illustrative churn rates, and the prescribed finite searches; neither the seed-bootstrap nor the market $t$ intervals cover search uncertainty. Within this model and at the tested rates, R3's measured advantage over C$^*$ does not extend past the two lowest churn rates of the earlier follow-up, and its pooled advantage over uniform audits lasts through $p = \const{0.03}$ but not to \const{0.05}. On the new markets the pooled point estimates agree, but at \const{0.03} the comparison with C$^*$ is mixed across markets.
