Prospective replication on new markets. After two external reviews of this section, we
planned a replication before any of its outcomes existed: the plan, its five expectations, the analysis
code, and its tests were committed, timestamped with OpenTimestamps, and published, and six
new markets (rep0 to rep5) were then drawn from a later Bitcoin block by the second test’s market
generator. The replication runs R3, C∗ , B3, and the runner-up comparator C-thr(12) at p = 0, 0.03,
and 0.05, with D2+H3 attacks searched on the new markets, each rule’s selected no-churn reference
candidate replayed under the same churn, the registered evaluation and doubling check in every cell,
and continued search of up to three rounds, under the same churn, for every cell whose doubling
check was flagged. Of the 72 cells, 20 were flagged and continued (6 at p = 0, 6 at 0.03, and 8 at
0.05; 8 of them R3’s), and every continued cell passed the continuation check within three rounds;
passing that check is not a claim of convergence. The primary analysis uses each cell’s frozen winner
unless its doubling check was flagged, and the continued winner otherwise; the all-frozen set is a
sensitivity analysis. The support criterion for a contrast at a rate is descriptive: its mean paired
change from the same market’s no-churn value is below zero, and the change is negative on at least
five of the six markets; it is not a significance test. Its material companion requires a mean change
at or below the registered Q1 margin of −0.05; both are reported for both winner sets. The fourth
expectation carried a stated prospective probability of 0.5. For the fifth, R3’s audited share of the
attacker’s jobs is the number of the attacker’s audited jobs over its served jobs, each summed over
a market’s 200 evaluation seeds; a market where the attacker serves no job at either rate counts as
not lower. Seed-bootstrap intervals are 95% paired percentile intervals from 9999 resamples (seed
0), resampling seeds within each market; market t intervals use 5 degrees of freedom. We call the
replication prospective-exploratory: its plan was fixed and stamped before its markets existed, but
it is not part of either registered test and changes no registered decision.



                                                  16
Replication results. Table 10 gives the paired changes and the absolute contrasts; Appendix B
gives them per market, with the attack and reference terms, the efficiency of every rule, and R3’s
audit allocation. With the primary winners all five expectations are met: the support criterion holds
for C∗ minus R3 at p = 0.05 (“met”), for B3 minus R3 at 0.05 (“met”), for both at 0.03 (“met”),
and for the runner-up minus R3 at 0.05 (“met”), and R3’s audited share of the attacker’s jobs is
lower at 0.05 than without churn on at least five of six markets (“met”). The mean paired changes
are −0.146 (d), −0.142 (q), and −0.118 (u) at p = 0.05, and −0.063, −0.065, and −0.051 at 0.03.
The material companion holds for 6 of the six contrast and rate pairs with the primary winners
and for 6 with the all-frozen winners; the support criterion holds for 6 and 5. These means rest
mainly on two markets: at p = 0.05 with the primary winners, d changes by −0.352 and −0.344
on rep0 and rep1, and by between −0.072 and −0.015 on the other four; with the primary winners
the other contrasts and the rate 0.03 show the same pattern (Table 30). The material companion
therefore describes the mean change, not a typical market. With the primary winners, the only
market t interval of a mean change that excludes zero is that of B3 minus R3 at 0.05 ([−0.282,
−0.003]); with the all-frozen winners the three intervals at 0.05 exclude zero and none at 0.03 does.
With the all-frozen winners, the first, second, fourth, and fifth expectations are met (“met”, “met”,
“met”, “met”), and the third is “not met”: C∗ minus R3 at 0.03 is lower than without churn on only
4 of six markets, so it fails the support criterion while meeting the material companion. Continued
search moved both the no-churn baselines and the churn cells: the pooled C∗ minus R3 difference
with the primary winners minus that with the all-frozen winners is −0.023 without churn, −0.034
at p = 0.03, and 0.046 at 0.05. With the all-frozen winners the mean paired changes at 0.05 are
−0.214 (d), −0.175 (q), and −0.156 (u), further from zero than with the primary winners: at this
rate the better-searched winner set gives the weaker changes. The paired changes therefore carry
the search uncertainty of the baselines as well as that of the churn cells. As planned, the failed
expectation is reported without a causal reading.

Ordering and audits on the new markets. At p = 0.05 with the primary winners, C∗ minus
R3 is −0.124 and negative on 6 of six markets, the runner-up minus R3 is −0.112 (negative on
6), and B3 minus R3 is −0.030, negative on 6 markets (5 with the all-frozen winners). The sign
reversals of the earlier follow-up thus recur on the new markets in point estimates, but with the
primary winners all three market t intervals of these absolute contrasts include zero (Table 10);
the pooled seed-bootstrap interval of B3 minus R3, which holds the six markets fixed, is [−0.034,
−0.027] (Table 31). At p = 0.03, C∗ minus R3 is −0.041 ([−0.115, 0.033]) and negative on only 4 of
six markets (3 with the all-frozen winners), against all six under both search versions in the earlier
follow-up; the new markets do not repeat that result. R3’s audited share of the attacker’s jobs falls
from between 0.655 and 0.952 without churn to between 0.012 and 0.201 at p = 0.05, and is lower
on 6 of six markets. At p = 0.05, honest identities younger than ten rounds serve 0.388 of the jobs
and receive 0.818 of R3’s audits, and established honest jobs are audited at 0.073 (equal-weight
means over the six markets; Table 33). At p = 0.03 the primary allocation covers only 3 of six
markets, because the plan ran diagnostics for continued R3 winners only at p = 0 and 0.05. Under
its selected attack at 0.05, R3’s mean gross efficiency is 0.970 (lowest market 0.929) and C∗ ’s is
0.993 (Table 32).

Scope. The earlier follow-up ran one verified-history comparator under churn; the replication
adds the runner-up, and on the new markets the deterioration under its descriptive criterion is
not specific to C∗ . Every claim is restricted to the generator’s market neighbourhood, the four
rules, the D2+H3 family, illustrative churn rates, and the prescribed finite searches; neither the


